<form method="post">
<select multiple name="test">
<option value="">placeholder</option>
<option value="1">a</option>
<option value="2">b</option>
</select>
</form>

router.post('/test', async (req, res, next) => {
    return res.send({body: req.body});
});

qs 6.14.0 shows : {"body":{"test":["1","2"]}}
qs 6.14.1 shows : {"body":{"test":{"0":"1","1":"2"}}}
```


---
[c7] @kroleg 2026-01-06T12:43:29Z:
Issue is closed but discussion is ongoing. Imo it's not a vulnerability at all (see https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/537#issuecomment-3713910862) and i am trying to convince maintainer to revert this breaking change. So far can't even convince him that it's breaking change :-/
---
[c8] @rafipiccolo 2026-01-06T12:49:31Z:
I guess i'm gonna use this from now on : app.use(express.urlencoded({ limit: '50mb', extended: true}));
since it returns real arrays as before.
So the breaking will be less breaking...
i'll still need to check the naming of the array in the htlm since name="test[]" will allways return arrays while name="test" will return arrays only when multiple values.
---
[c9] @UlisesGascon 2026-01-06T13:36:20Z:
> So far can't even convince him that it's breaking change :-/

I personally agree with the maintainers point of view expressed here ([ref](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/537#issuecomment-3711952472)).


This will be solved tomorrow as we plan to publish `body-parser@2.2.2` ([ref](https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser/pull/691)) with a specific compatibility patch with qs@6.14.1. So Express@5.x will work as expected out-of-the-box 👍 

>Is there a recommended way to keep the default behavior of getting arrays instead of objects when posting arrays ?

In the meantime one _possible workaround_ is to use `overrides` in the `package.json` ([docs](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v9/configuring-npm/package-json#overrides)) like:

```json
{
  "overrides": {
    "express": {
      "qs": "6.14.0"
    },
    "body-parser": {
      "qs": "6.14.0"
    },
  }
}
``` 

But then you will get the CVE alert (as you installing a known vulnerable package) 🤔 
---
[c10] @krzysdz 2026-01-06T13:37:50Z:
> Is there a recommended way to keep the default behavior of getting arrays instead of objects when posting arrays ?

Wait for `body-parser` v2.2.2 (https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser/pull/691), which includes a fix (https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser/pull/689) - in `"simple"` mode the `arrayLimit` is now set to `paramCount`, which is at most `parameterLimit` (1000 for `"simple"`).

In `package.json` the version is specified as `^2.2.1`, so once 2.2.2 is released it should be installed by `npm update` or `npm install`



---
[c11] @UlisesGascon 2026-01-07T09:59:10Z:
body-parser@2.2.2 was released recently ([changelog](https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser/releases/tag/v2.2.2)) solving the issue.
---
[c12] @forivall 2026-01-10T04:54:49Z:
For the issue of the small default `arrayLength` of 20, my solution is to just manually change the `query parser`:

```js
app.set('query parser', (string) =>
  qs.parse(string, {
    arrayLimit: 1000,
    throwOnLimitExceeded: true
  })
)
```

or use whatever options you need for your use case. This solves the largest problem described in https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/537 . (As for other libraries, they should expose the option in whatever manner is appropriate for their use case)
---
[c13] @bjohansebas 2026-01-10T14:23:24Z:
For those, just update their `package-lock` or run `npm update express body-parser` and they would already have the patch.
---
[c14] @jpblair 2026-01-15T20:34:44Z:
Echoing the question from @francescorivola: is the 5.x fix going to be ported back to 4.x, or is our best path to use @forivall's suggestion of manually overriding the `arrayLimit` value?
---
[c15] @krzysdz 2026-01-15T22:02:55Z:
> Echoing the question from [@francescorivola](https://github.com/francescorivola): is the 5.x fix going to be ported back to 4.x, or is our best path to use [@forivall](https://github.com/forivall)'s suggestion of manually overriding the `arrayLimit` value?

5.x required an update to [`body-parser`](https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser), which is exported as [`express.urlencoded()`](https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#express.urlencoded) middleware and for the non-extended mode switched from `querystring` to `qs` in 5.x.

When it comes to the `"extended"` `query parser` used for parsing query string into `req.query`, both Express 4 and 5 set only `allowPrototypes: true` and otherwise use the default settings. This is something that tests do not cover, but in both versions long query strings with over 20 array elements without indexes (e.g. `param=1&param=2` or `param[]=1&param[]=2`, but **not `param[0]=1&param[1]=2`**) will change into objects after updating `qs` (there is also another bug here - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/540).

I guess for now if you need such arrays in queries, you have to use a custom `"query parser"` in Express 4 **and 5**.

<details>
<summary>Demo</summary>

```js
const util = require("node:util");
const express = require("express");

const N = 22;

const app = express();
app.set("query parser", "extended");
app.get("/", (req, res) => {
	console.log(util.inspect(req.query, { compact: true, breakLength: Infinity, colors: true }));
	res.send("");
});

const server = app.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", async () => {
	const { port } = server.address();
	// this has never worked because of arrayLimit
	const q1 = Array(N).fill().map((_, i) => `param[${i}]=val`).join("&");
	await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/?${q1}`);
	// this works with qs@6.14.0, but doesn't with 6.14.1, which enforces arrayLimit on all arrays
	const q2 = Array(N).fill("param[]=val").join("&");
	await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/?${q2}`);
	server.close();
});

```

</details>
---
